Article 3 Monopolistic conduct as stipulated by this Law includes:
(1) Monopoly agreements reached by business operators;
(2) Abuse of market dominance by business operators;
(3) Concentration of business operators that has or may have the effect of eliminating or restricting competition.

Article 12 The State Council shall establish the Anti-Monopoly Commission, which shall be responsible for organizing, coordinating, and guiding anti-monopoly work, and perform the following duties:
(1) Studying and drafting relevant competition policies;
(2) Organizing investigations and assessments of the overall competition situation in the market, and issuing assessment reports;
(3) Formulating and issuing anti-monopoly guidelines;
(4) Coordinating anti-monopoly administrative law enforcement work;
(5) Other duties as prescribed by the State Council.
The composition and working rules of the Anti-Monopoly Commission of the State Council shall be prescribed by the State Council.

Article 17 Competing business operators shall be prohibited from reaching the following monopoly agreements:
(1) Fixing or changing the price of goods;
(2) Restricting the quantity of goods produced or sold;
(3) Dividing sales markets or raw material procurement markets;
(4) Restricting the purchase of new technologies or new equipment, or restricting the development of new technologies or new products;
(5) Jointly boycotting transactions;
(6) Other monopoly agreements as determined by the anti-monopoly law enforcement agency under the State Council.

Article 18 Business operators and their trading counterparties shall be prohibited from reaching the following monopoly agreements:
(1) Fixing the price of goods resold to a third party;
(2) Restricting the minimum price of goods resold to a third party;
(3) Other monopoly agreements as determined by the anti-monopoly law enforcement agency under the State Council.
Agreements provided for in Items (1) and (2) of the preceding paragraph shall not be prohibited if the business operator can prove that they do not have the effect of eliminating or restricting competition.
If a business operator can prove that its market share in the relevant market is below the standard prescribed by the anti-monopoly law enforcement agency under the State Council and meets other conditions prescribed by the anti-monopoly law enforcement agency under the State Council, it shall not be prohibited.

Article 20 Where a business operator can prove that the agreement reached falls under any of the following circumstances, the provisions of Articles 17, the first paragraph of Article 18, and Article 19 of this Law shall not apply:
(1) For the purpose of improving technology or researching and developing new products;
(2) For the purpose of improving product quality, reducing costs, enhancing efficiency, unifying product specifications or standards, or implementing specialization;
(3) For the purpose of improving the operational efficiency of small and medium-sized business operators and enhancing their competitiveness;
(4) For the purpose of achieving social public interests such as energy conservation, environmental protection, disaster relief, and assistance;
(5) For the purpose of alleviating a serious decline in sales volume or obvious overproduction due to economic downturn;
(6) For the purpose of safeguarding legitimate interests in foreign trade and foreign economic cooperation;
(7) Other circumstances as provided by law or prescribed by the State Council.
Where the circumstances provided for in Items (1) to (5) of the preceding paragraph apply and the provisions of Article 17, the first paragraph of Article 18, and Article 19 of this Law are not applicable, the business operator shall also prove that the agreement reached will not seriously restrict competition in the relevant market and can enable consumers to share the benefits arising therefrom.

Article 22 Business operators with a dominant market position are prohibited from engaging in the following abusive conduct of a dominant market position:
(1) selling products at unfairly high prices or purchasing products at unfairly low prices;
(2) without justifiable grounds, selling products at prices below cost;
(3) without justifiable grounds, refusing to deal with counterparties;
(4) without justifiable grounds, limiting counterparties to transact only with itself or only with designated business operators;
(5) without justifiable grounds, tying products or imposing other unreasonable trading conditions at the time of transaction;
(6) without justifiable grounds, applying differential treatment to counterparties with identical conditions in terms of transaction price or other transaction terms;
(7) other abusive conduct of a dominant market position as determined by the anti-monopoly enforcement authority under the State Council.
Business operators with a dominant market position shall not use data, algorithms, technology, platform rules, etc. to engage in the abusive conduct of a dominant market position as specified in the preceding paragraph.
For the purposes of this Law, "dominant market position" means a market position in which a business operator has the ability to control the price, quantity, or other transaction terms of products in the relevant market, or to impede or affect the ability of other business operators to enter the relevant market.

Article 23 The determination that a business operator has a dominant market position shall be based on the following factors:
(1) the market share of the business operator in the relevant market and the competition conditions of the relevant market;
(2) the ability of the business operator to control the sales market or the raw material procurement market;
(3) the financial and technological conditions of the business operator;
(4) the degree of dependence of other business operators on the business operator in transactions;
(5) the degree of difficulty for other business operators to enter the relevant market;
(6) other factors relevant to the determination of the business operator's dominant market position.

Article 24 A business operator may be presumed to have a dominant market position in any of the following circumstances:
(1) the market share of one business operator in the relevant market reaches one-half;
(2) the combined market share of two business operators in the relevant market reaches two-thirds;
(3) the combined market share of three business operators in the relevant market reaches three-fourths.
Where the circumstances specified in item (2) or (3) of the preceding paragraph exist, and the market share of one of the business operators is less than one-tenth, that business operator shall not be presumed to have a dominant market position.
Where a business operator presumed to have a dominant market position has evidence that it does not have a dominant market position, it shall not be deemed to have a dominant market position.

Article 25 Concentration of business operators means any of the following circumstances:
(1) merger of business operators;
(2) acquisition of control over other business operators by purchasing equities or assets;
(3) acquisition of control over other business operators or the ability to exert decisive influence over other business operators through contracts or other means.

Article 27 A concentration of business operators may be exempt from notification to the anti-monopoly enforcement authority under the State Council in any of the following circumstances:
(1) one business operator participating in the concentration owns more than fifty percent of the voting shares or assets of each other business operator in the concentration;
(2) more than fifty percent of the voting shares or assets of each business operator participating in the concentration are owned by a single business operator not participating in the concentration.

Article 28 When filing a notification of concentration with the anti-monopoly enforcement authority under the State Council, a business operator shall submit the following documents and materials:
(1) a notification letter;
(2) a description of the impact of the concentration on the competition conditions in the relevant market;
(3) the concentration agreement;
(4) the financial and accounting reports of the business operators participating in the concentration for the previous accounting year, audited by an accounting firm;
(5) other documents and materials prescribed by the anti-monopoly enforcement authority under the State Council.
The notification letter shall include the names, domiciles, business scopes of the business operators participating in the concentration, the intended date of implementation of the concentration, and other matters prescribed by the anti-monopoly enforcement authority under the State Council.

Article 31 Where the anti-monopoly enforcement authority under the State Council decides to conduct further review, it shall complete the review within ninety days from the date of such decision, make a decision on whether to prohibit the concentration, and inform the business operator in writing. Any decision prohibiting a concentration shall state the reasons. During the review period, the business operator shall not implement the concentration.
In any of the following circumstances, the anti-monopoly enforcement authority under the State Council may extend the review period specified in the preceding paragraph upon written notice to the business operator, provided that the extension shall not exceed sixty days:
(1) the business operator agrees to the extension of the review period;
(2) the documents and materials submitted by the business operator are inaccurate and require further verification;
(3) a significant change occurs in the relevant circumstances after the notification.
If the anti-monopoly enforcement authority under the State Council fails to make a decision within the time limit, the business operator may implement the concentration.

Article 32 In any of the following circumstances, the anti-monopoly enforcement authority under the State Council may decide to suspend the calculation of the review period for a concentration and inform the business operator in writing:
(1) the business operator fails to submit documents and materials as required, making the review impossible;
(2) new circumstances or facts that have a material impact on the review of the concentration arise, and the review would be impossible without verification;
(3) further assessment of restrictive conditions to be imposed on the concentration is necessary, and the business operator requests suspension.
Upon elimination of the circumstances causing the suspension of the calculation of the review period, the review period shall resume, and the anti-monopoly enforcement authority under the State Council shall inform the business operator in writing.

Article 33 The following factors shall be considered in the review of concentrations of business operators:
(1) the market shares of the business operators participating in the concentration in the relevant market and their ability to control the market;
(2) the market concentration of the relevant market;
(3) the impact of the concentration on market entry and technological progress;
(4) the impact of the concentration on consumers and other relevant business operators;
(5) the impact of the concentration on the development of the national economy;
(6) other factors affecting market competition that the anti-monopoly enforcement authority under the State Council considers necessary to consider.

Article 41 An administrative organ or an organization authorized by law or regulation to perform functions of public affairs administration shall not abuse its administrative power to engage in any of the following conducts that impede the free circulation of commodities between regions:
(1) Imposing discriminatory charges, implementing discriminatory charging standards, or prescribing discriminatory prices on commodities from other regions;
(2) Prescribing technical requirements or inspection standards for commodities from other regions that are different from those for similar local commodities, or adopting discriminatory technical measures such as repeated inspection or repeated certification on commodities from other regions to restrict their entry into the local market;
(3) Adopting administrative licenses specifically targeting commodities from other regions to restrict their entry into the local market;
(4) Setting up checkpoints or taking other means to obstruct the entry of commodities from other regions or the exit of local commodities; or
(5) Any other conduct that impedes the free circulation of commodities between regions.

Article 47 The anti-monopoly law enforcement agency may take the following measures when investigating suspected monopolistic conducts:
(1) Entering the business premises of the business operator under investigation or other relevant places for inspection;
(2) Inquiring the business operator under investigation, interested parties, or other relevant entities or individuals, and requesting them to explain the relevant circumstances;
(3) Inspecting and copying relevant documents, agreements, accounting books, business correspondence, electronic data, and other documents and materials of the business operator under investigation, interested parties, or other relevant entities or individuals;
(4) Sealing up or seizing relevant evidence; and
(5) Inquiring into the bank accounts of the business operator.
Taking the measures specified in the preceding paragraph shall be subject to a written report to and approval from the principal responsible person of the anti-monopoly law enforcement agency.
